WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.205
- Do you think I could
pull off dark academia?

00:00:03.205 --> 00:00:05.730
- Mm, no.
(wrong buzzer beeps)

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:08.220
- What about cottagecore?

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:11.100
- We are not doing this again.
- Fine.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.820
Maybe you like me, haven't found
your perfect aesthetic yet.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:16.410
- Or maybe you got a new job

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:18.810
or you've hit that summer in your twenties

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:21.360
or you have eight different
weddings to go to.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:22.380
- For whatever reason,

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:23.790
you want a whole new wardrobe

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:25.770
and you want it right now, no problem.

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:28.710
You can open up a new Shein
tab or hit your local H&amp;M

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:32.130
and walk out with 10 different
outfits for under 200 bucks.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:34.350
- That seems better
than spending thousands

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:37.110
of dollars you don't
have on designer clothes.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:38.910
But what happens to that closet

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:41.610
full of new outfits a year from now?

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:43.171
What about in a month?

00:00:43.171 --> 00:00:45.754
(upbeat music)

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.120
- Critics of fast fashion
have long detailed

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:55.920
the industry's environmental costs

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.350
and unsavory labor practices,

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:00.510
but Americans still spend an average

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:04.410
of $1,700 per year on cheap new clothes.

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:06.660
So how did fashion start cycling

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.810
through our wardrobes
and our wallets so fast?

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:11.280
- As recently as the 1980s,

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:12.900
most fashion brands brought out

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.510
new collections two to four times a year,

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:17.460
giving consumers new pieces to buy

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.470
with the changing of the seasons.

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:22.320
The average American
bought about 12 new pieces

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:23.820
of clothing annually.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:26.250
- But in order to increase their profits

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:28.380
clothing companies wanted to figure out

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.870
how to get shoppers to buy more.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:33.810
They figured out two
reliable ways to do that,

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:36.360
lower prices and more options.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.810
By the early two thousands
fashion brands like Zara, H&amp;M,

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:41.760
and Forever 21 figured out

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.350
that they could increase the demand

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:46.320
for new clothing items
by making more of them.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.600
Instead of coming out
with clothes twice a year

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:49.860
they were putting new items

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.860
on their retail floors every two weeks.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.120
- And the cheaper the items

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.070
the more comfortable shoppers would be

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:58.170
with making another fashion haul.

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:01.170
In order to keep prices
low and profits high

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:05.400
fast fashion retailers started
skimping out on everything

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.980
from the wages of the
people who sew your garments

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:10.860
to the quality of the
fabric and construction.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:13.320
Everything from the way
they cut pattern pieces

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:16.260
to the zippers and buttons
they use is designed

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:19.710
to keep production costs
as low as possible.

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:22.890
- These frequent collections
also warp our perspective

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:25.200
on how much clothing is enough.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.560
Yes, everyone wears
something to work every day.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:29.760
- [Julia] At least we hope.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.070
- But fast fashion retailers
want us to feel like

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:35.280
we need to go all in on
the latest TikTok aesthetic

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.770
even if the trend will already be passe

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:39.840
by the time our order arrives.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:42.900
The pace of fast fashion
has escalated so much

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.170
that online retailers like
Shein now put thousands

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.690
of new items on their site every day

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:52.470
and the average American
buys 68 new items a year.

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:56.010
- We also throw away way more clothes.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:59.610
Because brands cut so many
corners to keep prices low,

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.940
your clothes are literally
built to fall apart

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:06.090
and all the better for
fast fashion's bottom line.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:08.100
If consumers buy into the idea

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:10.260
that their clothes are disposable

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:12.870
they'll keep coming back to buy more.

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:15.630
Who cares if it's so affordable, right?

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:17.010
- Well, it's not affordable

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:18.900
to the people making your clothes.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:21.450
As Aja Barber writes
in her book "Consumed"

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:23.430
about the impacts of fast fashion,

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:25.740
the average garment worker can't afford

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:27.930
to buy the clothes she makes.

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:30.660
Retailers like Shein
regularly come under fire

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.030
for violating labor
laws in their factories

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:35.730
where garment workers put in 16 hour days,

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:37.800
generally making about 4 cents

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:39.660
per finished item of clothing.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:42.510
- And it's not affordable
for the planet either!

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:43.710
According to the EPA,

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:47.430
Americans throw out over 11 million tons

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:49.500
of textiles every year.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:52.740
That's five times what we
tossed out in the eighties!

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:55.130
Now, fast fashion makes up 8%

00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:57.450
of the world's greenhouse gas emissions

00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:00.300
in the cycle from production to landfill.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:02.700
- Because the trend
cycles are moving so fast

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:04.410
and the clothing is made poorly,

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:07.230
your fast fashion likely can't be donated

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:09.930
or even worn for more than a few wears.

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:11.520
It's just trash.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:14.790
The low prices of fast
fashion obscure the true cost

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:17.880
to workers, consumers,
and the environment.

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:19.620
- But even if fast fashion wasn't bad

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:21.510
for the people or the planet

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:24.960
it can still be pretty
disastrous for your wallet.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:28.050
In our episode about how
it's expensive to be poor

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:30.780
we talked about how
buying the cheapest option

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:33.000
can cost more in the long run.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.440
And that's true here too.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:35.940
- If you buy the average number

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:37.680
of fast fashion pieces a year,

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:40.890
about 68 and spend $20 per item,

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:44.940
over five years you will
have spent $6,800 on clothes,

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:46.560
maybe more if you put all those clothes

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:48.210
on a credit card you can't pay off

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:51.090
or bought them using a
buy now pay later service.

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:52.950
- If you return to the average number

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:55.440
of garments someone bought in the 1980s

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:58.530
you could even spend
four times as much money

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:00.930
per piece on higher quality items.

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:02.580
And over five years

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:06.600
you'll have spent $2,000
less on clothes overall.

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:09.120
And because you bought clothes that last

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.790
by now you've got just as many items

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:14.220
as the average fast fashion shopper.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:17.910
- How can more expensive
clothing turn out to be cheaper?

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:19.650
Consider cost per wear

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:21.720
by dividing the price of a clothing item

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:23.730
by the number of times you'll use it.

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:26.070
A $15 dress you wear twice

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:28.920
works out to $7 each time you wear it.

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:33.150
Meanwhile, a $100 dress that
lasts you three dozen wears

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:35.760
comes out to less than $3 per wear.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:38.760
Fast fashion seems more
affordable in the short term,

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:39.990
but even though it feels

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:41.550
like you're getting way more clothes,

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:44.310
you're getting a lot
less value out of them.

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:46.320
- So, what can you do instead?

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:49.830
First, buy less and buy better.

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:53.910
Building a wardrobe you love
is a marathon, not a sprint.

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:55.920
So take your time when you shop

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:59.460
and learn to check seams, hems, linings,

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:01.470
and other details that help you know

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:03.690
if a garment is built to last.

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:07.470
Higher prices don't always mean
the garment is better made,

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:10.350
but if you wanna splurge on
some higher quality items

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:12.630
you get more out of your clothing budget

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:14.700
if you buy fewer pieces per year.

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:17.220
- Buying less might mean you feel left out

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:20.220
when everyone else is having
their disco pants era.

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:22.920
But the whole reason trends
exist in the first place is

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:26.250
because clothing manufacturers
are churning out new items

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:29.010
and running ads and
pushing sponsored content

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:32.880
into your feeds to get you
to spend more of your money.

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:35.880
Remember, you don't have
to buy into every trend

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:37.800
in order to have personal style.

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:41.160
- My personal favorite
solution is buying secondhand.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:44.280
It lets you divert someone
else's waste from the landfill

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:47.310
in the process of getting
you your new favorite fit.

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:50.250
And for special events or costume parties

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:52.380
consider borrowing or renting

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:54.330
rather than buying a fancy outfit

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:56.100
you only plan to wear once.

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:59.400
- And finally, treat the
clothes that you have with care.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.440
Even if you already own fast fashion,

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:03.000
taking good care of your clothes

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:04.830
gives them a longer lifespan.

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:06.720
Read those annoying little labels

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:09.090
so you know when to
hand wash delicate items

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:10.710
or hang things to air dry.

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:13.980
- Watch YouTube tutorials and
learn how to do basic mending

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:15.780
like fixing a loose hem,

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:18.300
sewing on a button or patching a hole.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:21.090
Visible mending techniques
that use colorful thread

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:24.780
or fun patches are also a
great way to update an old item

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:25.740
for a new look.

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:28.170
- Lots of shoppers say
they buy fast fashion

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:29.910
because they want to have fun

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:32.400
with their clothes without
spending too much money.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:33.233
- And we get it.

00:07:33.233 --> 00:07:34.890
No matter what your budget,

00:07:34.890 --> 00:07:37.410
you deserve to love the clothes you wear.

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:40.920
But to get there, we all
need to treat our wardrobes

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:43.860
like treasures, not like trash.

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:45.690
- [Both] And that's our 2 cents.

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:48.810
